          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#cartesian_test_spaces_diffeological_spaces_and_smooth_algebras Cartesian test spaces: diffeological spaces and smooth algebras
          %p
            Consider the category of test spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo =
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C =
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CartSp"} CartSp
          %p Then
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                spaces modeled on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                are
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+smooth+space"} generalized smooth spaces
                such as
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeological+space"} diffeological spaces
            %li
              %p
                quantities modeled on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                are
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+algebra"} smooth algebras
                (
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty
                \-rings).
          %p
            The adjunction
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒪
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi Spec
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathcal{O} \dashv Spec)
            sends a smooth space to its smooth algebra of functions and a smooth algebra of functions to its “spectrum”.
          %h2#higher_space_and_higher_quantity Higher space and higher quantity
          %p There are various specializations of interest on this
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher categorical
                version
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-space"} ∞-space
                    modeled on
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi C
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                    is a
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+presheaf"} simplicial presheaf
                    on
                    = succeed "," do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi C
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi X
